data.content:
The lightest option for modern .NET (8+) is the **built-in `TimeProvider`** class — no packages, no DI container needed. Just accept it as a constructor parameter with a sensible default:

```csharp
namespace InvoiceLib;

public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
    {
        _time = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;
    }

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

**In tests**, use `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider` (from the `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` NuGet package):

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
fake.SetUtcNow(...); // advance or rewind at will
```

**Why this is the lightest approach:**
- `TimeProvider` ships in the BCL (.NET 8+) — zero production dependencies
- The optional parameter means existing callers don't break
- No interface to define, no DI container to wire up

